    {
     "work_id": "W7164528628",
     "title": "Developmental Changes in Children's Production of Line Drawings in Global Contexts: Evidence from Kisumu, Kenya",
     "url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/desc.70316",
     "flair": "Judith Fan",
     "authors": "Rebecca Zhu, Joab Ochieng Arieda, Tabitha Nduku, Zachary Watson and 4 others",
     "publisher": "Developmental Science",
     "published": "2026-11-25",
     "forthcoming": false,
     "type": "article",
     "excerpt": "Children growing up in environments with many drawing opportunities improve dramatically at producing recognizable drawings over the course of childhood. Does this same developmental trajectory hold for children growing up in environments with fewer drawing opportunities? We measured children's…",
     "abstract": "Children growing up in environments with many drawing opportunities improve dramatically at producing recognizable drawings over the course of childhood. Does this same developmental trajectory hold for children growing up in environments with fewer drawing opportunities? We measured children's ability to produce recognizable drawings in a context where children have relatively fewer drawing opportunities and experiences with visual media, specifically Kisumu, Kenya. By leveraging within-context variation in children's drawing experience, we estimated the strength of the association between how often a child draws and how recognizable their drawings are. A preregistered sample of 120 4- to 9-year-olds drew 12 object categories in random order. Children produced more recognizable drawings with age, even after controlling for their tracing abilities. Moreover, younger children with more drawing experience produced more recognizable drawings, even after controlling for tracing ability and other home environment variables. These results suggest commonalities in drawing development across cultures and document the experience-dependence of drawing development in global contexts.",
     "score": null,
     "summary": "Researchers asked whether children in a setting with fewer opportunities to draw show the age-related gains in drawing seen elsewhere. In Kisumu, Kenya, they asked 120 children aged 4 to 9 to draw objects and compared how recognizable the drawings were with the children’s ages and drawing experience. Older children made more recognizable drawings, even after accounting for tracing ability, and younger children with more drawing experience also made more recognizable drawings after accounting for tracing ability and other home factors. The findings point to similarities in drawing development across cultures and a link between drawing experience and children’s skill."
    }
   ]
